/* ========== GCSE PHYSICS (AQA 8463) · LEVEL 2 · Year 10 ========== */
U({id:'gp2-1',track:'gp',level:2,title:'Energy',icon:'E',color:'#D85A30',desc:'Energy stores and transfers, kinetic, gravitational and elastic energy, specific heat capacity, power, efficiency and energy resources.',lessons:[
LH('gp2-1-1','Energy stores and kinetic energy',9,'Energy is never used up, only moved between stores. Meet the stores, and the first equation you must learn.',[
T(`<h3>Energy stores</h3><p>Energy is stored in different ways. Physicists call them <b>stores</b>:</p><ul><li><b>Kinetic</b>: anything moving</li><li><b>Gravitational potential</b>: anything raised up</li><li><b>Elastic potential</b>: anything stretched or squashed</li><li><b>Thermal</b> (internal): anything hot</li><li><b>Chemical</b>: food, fuel, batteries</li><li><b>Magnetic</b>, <b>electrostatic</b> and <b>nuclear</b></li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>Moving energy between stores</h3><p>Energy is <b>transferred</b> from one store to another in four ways:</p><ul><li><b>Mechanically</b>: a force moves something (doing work)</li><li><b>Electrically</b>: a current flows</li><li><b>By heating</b></li><li><b>By radiation</b>: light, sound and other waves</li></ul><p>Throw a ball up: energy moves from its <b>kinetic</b> store to its <b>gravitational potential</b> store as it slows down and rises.</p>`),
W('sortIt',{bins:['Kinetic','Gravitational potential','Chemical','Thermal'],items:[['A moving car',0],['A sprinting athlete',0],['A rollercoaster at the top of the track',1],['A book on a high shelf',1],['A battery',2],['Petrol',2],['A cup of hot tea',3],['A hot radiator',3]]},`Which store is the energy mainly in? Sort them.`,`Moving: kinetic. High up: gravitational potential. Fuel and batteries: chemical. Hot: thermal.`),
Q(`You drop a ball. Which energy transfer happens as it falls?`,[`Gravitational potential → kinetic`,`Kinetic → gravitational potential`,`Chemical → kinetic`,`Thermal → kinetic`],0,`It loses height (gravitational potential) and speeds up (kinetic).`),
T(`<h3>Kinetic energy</h3><p>The energy in an object's kinetic store depends on its mass and its speed:</p><div class="eq">E<sub>k</sub> = ½ m v²</div>${KY([['E<sub>k</sub>','kinetic energy','J'],['m','mass','kg'],['v','speed','m/s']],'Your exams (summer 2028) come with an equation sheet, but learn it anyway: knowing it by heart makes you much faster.')}`),
T(`<h3>Speed counts double</h3><p>Speed is <b>squared</b>. That means:</p><ul><li>Double the mass → double the kinetic energy.</li><li>Double the speed → <b>four times</b> the kinetic energy (2² = 4).</li></ul><p>That's why a crash at 60 mph is so much worse than one at 30 mph.</p>`),
Q(`A car doubles its speed. What happens to its kinetic energy?`,[`It becomes 4 times bigger`,'It becomes 2 times bigger','It becomes 8 times bigger','It becomes 4 times smaller'],0,`E<sub>k</sub> depends on v². (2v)² = 4v².`),
W('stepCalc',{steps:[`A 1200 kg car moves at 10 m/s. Find its kinetic energy.`,`Write the equation: E<sub>k</sub> = ½ m v²`,`Square the speed first: 10² = 100`,`Multiply: 0.5 × 1200 × 100`,`E<sub>k</sub> = <b>60 000 J</b> (that's 60 kJ)`]},`A worked example, one step at a time.`,`Square the speed before you multiply.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_ke',need:5},`Your turn. Get 5 right to master it. Some ask for the speed.`,`To find v: v² = 2E<sub>k</sub> ÷ m, then square-root.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Energy is stored in <b>stores</b> and moved by <b>transfers</b>.</li><li>Energy is never used up, only moved.</li><li><b>E<sub>k</sub> = ½mv²</b>. Learn it.</li><li>Double the speed, four times the kinetic energy.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp2-1-2','Gravitational and elastic energy',9,'Lifting things and stretching springs stores energy. Two more equations, and what happens when energy changes store.',[
RM(`<p>Kinetic energy: <b>E<sub>k</sub> = ½mv²</b>, with m in kg and v in m/s. Energy moves between stores but is never used up.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Gravitational potential energy</h3><p>Lift something up and you fill its gravitational potential energy store:</p><div class="eq">E<sub>p</sub> = m g h</div>${KY([['E<sub>p</sub>','gravitational potential energy','J'],['m','mass','kg'],['g','gravitational field strength (9.8 on Earth)','N/kg'],['h','change in height','m']],'Learn this one too. AQA uses g = 9.8 N/kg.')}`),
N(`A 2 kg book is lifted 1.5 m onto a shelf. How much gravitational potential energy does it gain? (g = 9.8 N/kg)`,29.4,0.01,'J',`E<sub>p</sub> = mgh = 2 × 9.8 × 1.5 = 29.4 J.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_gpe',need:4},`Practise E<sub>p</sub> = mgh. Get 4 right to master it.`,`To find h, divide E<sub>p</sub> by (m × g).`),
T(`<h3>Elastic potential energy</h3><p>Stretch or squash a spring and you store energy in it:</p><div class="eq">E<sub>e</sub> = ½ k e²</div>${KY([['E<sub>e</sub>','elastic potential energy','J'],['k','spring constant (how stiff the spring is)','N/m'],['e','extension (how much longer it got)','m']],'This one is on the equation sheet. Always convert cm into m first: 5 cm = 0.05 m.')}`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_epe',need:3},`Practise E<sub>e</sub> = ½ke². Get 3 right to master it.`,`Convert cm to m by dividing by 100, then square it.`),
T(`<h3>When energy changes store</h3><p>Drop a ball and, ignoring air resistance, all the gravitational potential energy it loses becomes kinetic energy:</p><div class="eq">energy lost from E<sub>p</sub> = energy gained by E<sub>k</sub></div><p>This is <b>conservation of energy</b>. It lets you link two equations together.</p>`),
N(`A 0.5 kg ball is dropped from 5 m. Ignoring air resistance, how much kinetic energy does it have just before it lands? (g = 9.8 N/kg)`,24.5,0.01,'J',`All the E<sub>p</sub> becomes E<sub>k</sub>: mgh = 0.5 × 9.8 × 5 = 24.5 J.`),
Q(`Why do questions say "ignoring air resistance"?`,[`Air resistance transfers some energy to the thermal store of the surroundings`,'Air resistance adds extra energy to the kinetic store of the ball','Air pushes down on the ball and makes it heavier as it falls','Air changes the value of g, so the answer would no longer use g = 9.8 N/kg at all'],0,`With air resistance, some energy is wasted, so less ends up as kinetic energy.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li><b>E<sub>p</sub> = mgh</b> (learn it, g = 9.8 N/kg).</li><li><b>E<sub>e</sub> = ½ke²</b> (given). Convert cm to m.</li><li>Energy lost from one store = energy gained by another.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp2-1-3','Specific heat capacity',9,'Why water takes so long to heat up, the equation for it, and the required practical.',[
RM(`<p>Energy can be transferred <b>by heating</b> into an object's <b>thermal</b> store. Energy is measured in joules (J).</p>`),
T(`<h3>Some things heat up faster</h3><p>Put the same energy into 1 kg of water and 1 kg of aluminium. The aluminium gets much hotter. Water needs far more energy for each degree.</p><p>The <b>specific heat capacity</b> of a substance is the energy needed to raise the temperature of <b>1 kg</b> of it by <b>1 °C</b>.</p><p>Water's is 4200 J/kg °C, one of the highest there is. That's why water is used to carry heat around radiators, and to carry heat away from a nuclear reactor's core.</p>`),
T(`<h3>The equation</h3><div class="eq">ΔE = m c Δθ</div>${KY([['ΔE','change in thermal energy','J'],['m','mass','kg'],['c','specific heat capacity','J/kg °C'],['Δθ','temperature change (θ is "theta")','°C']],'This is on the equation sheet. Δ (delta) means "change in".')}`),
N(`How much energy is needed to heat 2 kg of water by 10 °C? (c = 4200 J/kg °C)`,84000,0.001,'J',`ΔE = mcΔθ = 2 × 4200 × 10 = 84 000 J.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_shc',need:5},`Practise ΔE = mcΔθ. Get 5 right to master it. Some ask you to rearrange.`,`Rearranged: Δθ = ΔE ÷ (mc), and c = ΔE ÷ (mΔθ).`),
T(`<h3>Required practical: specific heat capacity</h3><ol><li>Put a heater and a thermometer into holes in a 1 kg metal block.</li><li>Wrap the block in insulation.</li><li>Measure the energy supplied with a joulemeter (or E = VIt), and the temperature every minute.</li><li>Plot temperature rise against energy. Then c = energy ÷ (m × temperature rise).</li></ol>`),
Q(`In the specific heat capacity practical, some heat escapes to the surroundings. How does this affect the value of c you calculate?`,[`It comes out too high`,`It comes out too low`,`It has no effect`,`It comes out negative`],0,`More energy was supplied than actually went into the block for that temperature rise. Dividing a too-big energy gives a too-big c. Insulation reduces this.`),
Q(`Why put a little oil or water in the thermometer hole?`,[`To improve thermal contact, so the thermometer reads the block's temperature`,'To cool the block down so it doesn\'t get too hot to touch','To stop the block rusting, which would change its heat capacity and its mass over time','To make the heater work, because it needs a liquid to heat up'],0,`Air is a poor conductor; the liquid lets heat reach the thermometer properly.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Specific heat capacity: energy to heat 1 kg by 1 °C.</li><li><b>ΔE = mcΔθ</b> (given).</li><li>Water has a very high c: 4200 J/kg °C.</li><li>Heat loss in the practical makes c come out too high.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp2-1-4','Power, efficiency and energy resources',10,'How fast energy is transferred, how much is wasted, and where our energy comes from.',[
RM(`<p>Energy is conserved: it is never created or destroyed. ΔE = mcΔθ. E<sub>k</sub> = ½mv², E<sub>p</sub> = mgh.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Power</h3><p><b>Power</b> is how fast energy is transferred:</p><div class="eq">P = E ÷ t &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; P = W ÷ t</div>${KY([['P','power','W (watts)'],['E','energy transferred','J'],['W','work done','J'],['t','time','s']],'1 watt = 1 joule per second. Learn both. Always convert minutes into seconds.')}`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_power',need:4},`Practise power. Get 4 right to master it.`,`P = E ÷ t, or E = Pt. Time must be in seconds.`),
T(`<h3>Efficiency</h3><div class="eq">efficiency = useful output ÷ total input</div><p>You can use energy or power. Multiply by 100 for a percentage. Nothing is more than 100% efficient.</p><p>The rest of the energy is <b>wasted</b>: it spreads out (is <b>dissipated</b>), usually heating the surroundings.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_eff',need:4},`Practise efficiency. Get 4 right to master it.`,`Useful ÷ total, then × 100 for a percentage.`),
T(`<h3>Wasting less</h3><ul><li><b>Lubrication</b> (oil) reduces friction, so less energy heats moving parts.</li><li><b>Thermal insulation</b> slows down energy loss. Materials with a <b>low thermal conductivity</b> and <b>thicker walls</b> transfer energy more slowly.</li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>Energy resources</h3><p><b>Non-renewable</b> resources will run out: coal, oil, natural gas and nuclear fuel.</p><p><b>Renewable</b> resources are replaced naturally: wind, solar, hydroelectric, tidal, wave, geothermal and biofuel.</p><p>Each has pros and cons: how reliable it is, its cost, and its effect on the environment (CO₂, land use, waste, noise).</p>`),
W('sortIt',{bins:['Renewable','Non-renewable'],items:[['Wind',0],['Solar',0],['Tidal',0],['Geothermal',0],['Biofuel',0],['Coal',1],['Natural gas',1],['Uranium (nuclear fuel)',1],['Oil',1]]},`Sort the energy resources.`,`Nuclear fuel is non-renewable, but it gives out no CO₂ while generating.`),
Q(`Why is nuclear power useful even though it isn't renewable?`,[`It gives out no CO₂ while running and is reliable day and night`,'It is actually renewable, because uranium never runs out','It makes no waste at all, and runs whenever the wind blows','It is cheap and quick to build, and turns on in seconds'],0,`Its downsides are radioactive waste and high building costs, but it is low-carbon and reliable.`),
Q(`Which renewable resource is least reliable from hour to hour?`,[`Wind`,`Tidal`,`Geothermal`,`Hydroelectric`],0,`Tides are predictable, geothermal is constant, and hydro can be switched on when needed. The wind changes all the time.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li><b>P = E ÷ t</b> and <b>P = W ÷ t</b>. 1 W = 1 J/s.</li><li><b>Efficiency = useful ÷ total</b>.</li><li>Wasted energy is dissipated, usually as heat.</li><li>Renewable vs non-renewable: reliability, cost and environment.</li></ul>`)])
]});

U({id:'gp2-2',track:'gp',level:2,title:'Electricity',icon:'Ω',color:'#378ADD',desc:'Charge and current, potential difference and resistance, I–V graphs, series and parallel circuits, mains electricity, power and the National Grid.',lessons:[
LH('gp2-2-1','Current, charge and resistance',10,'What a current actually is, the circuit symbols you need, and V = IR.',[
T(`<h3>Circuit symbols</h3><p>You need to recognise and draw the standard symbols. Flip each card to see what it does.</p>`),
W('flipCards',{cards:[['Cell and battery','Push charge around the circuit. A battery is two or more cells.'],['Ammeter','Measures current. Always connected <b>in series</b>.'],['Voltmeter','Measures potential difference. Always connected <b>in parallel</b> across a component.'],['Resistor','Limits the current. A variable resistor can be adjusted.'],['Diode','Lets current flow in one direction only. An LED is a diode that gives out light.'],['Thermistor','Its resistance falls as it gets hotter.'],['LDR','Light-dependent resistor: its resistance falls in brighter light.'],['Fuse','Melts and breaks the circuit if the current gets too big.']]},`Flip all eight cards.`,`Ammeters in series, voltmeters in parallel: that comes up every year.`),
T(`<h3>Current is flowing charge</h3><p>An electric <b>current</b> is a flow of electric charge (in metal wires, electrons). It's the <b>rate of flow of charge</b>:</p><div class="eq">Q = I t</div>${KY([['Q','charge','C (coulombs)'],['I','current','A (amps)'],['t','time','s']],'Learn it. In a single closed loop, the current is the same everywhere.')}`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_charge',need:4},`Practise Q = It. Get 4 right to master it.`,`I = Q ÷ t to find the current.`),
T(`<h3>Potential difference and resistance</h3><p><b>Potential difference</b> (p.d., in volts) pushes the charge around. <b>Resistance</b> (in ohms, Ω) opposes the current. They're linked by:</p><div class="eq">V = I R</div>${KY([['V','potential difference','V (volts)'],['I','current','A'],['R','resistance','Ω (ohms)']],'Learn it. For a fixed p.d., more resistance means less current.')}`),
Q(`How must a voltmeter be connected?`,[`In parallel, across the component`,`In series with the component`,'In series, so all the current goes through it','In parallel, but only across the battery'],0,`It compares the "push" on either side of the component, so it goes across it.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_vir',need:6},`Practise V = IR all three ways round. Get 6 right to master it.`,`Cover the one you want: V = IR, I = V ÷ R, R = V ÷ I.`),
T(`<h3>Required practical: resistance of a wire</h3><p>Connect different lengths of wire, measure V and I for each, and calculate R = V ÷ I. Plot R against length: it's a straight line. <b>Longer wire, more resistance</b>, in proportion.</p><p>Use a small current and switch off between readings, so the wire doesn't heat up.</p>`),
Q(`In the resistance practical, why switch off between readings?`,[`The wire heats up, and that increases its resistance`,'To save the battery, so the voltage stays the same','The ammeter needs to reset to zero between readings','Otherwise the current starts to flow the wrong way round'],0,`Temperature is a control variable: a hotter wire has more resistance.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Current is the rate of flow of charge: <b>Q = It</b>.</li><li><b>V = IR</b>. Learn both.</li><li>Ammeter in series, voltmeter in parallel.</li><li>Longer wire, more resistance.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp2-2-2','I–V graphs, series and parallel',10,'How different components behave, sensors, and the rules for series and parallel circuits.',[
RM(`<p><b>V = IR</b>. Current is the same all round a single loop. Ammeter in series, voltmeter in parallel.</p>`),
T(`<h3>I–V characteristics</h3><p>Plot current (I) against potential difference (V) and different components give different shapes. This is a required practical.</p>`),
W('fnPlot',{x:[-6,6],y:[-1,1],xl:'V (volts)',yl:'I (amps)',sl:[['k','Component',1,3,1,1,function(v){return ['','Resistor','Filament lamp','Diode'][v]}]],curves:function(p){var F=[null,function(x){return x/8},function(x){return (x<0?-1:1)*Math.sqrt(Math.abs(x))*0.35},function(x){return x<0.6?0:Math.min(1,(x-0.6)*1.4)}];return [[F[p.k],'--accent',3]]},read:function(p){return ['','<b>Resistor</b> (at constant temperature): a straight line through the origin. Current is proportional to p.d. Its resistance stays the same. It is called an <b>ohmic conductor</b>.','<b>Filament lamp</b>: a curve that gets flatter. As the current increases the filament gets hotter, so its <b>resistance increases</b>.','<b>Diode</b>: no current one way, because the resistance in reverse is very high. Current flows only in the forward direction.'][p.k]}},`Slide through the three components and read each graph.`,`Straight line: fixed resistance. Curve: resistance changing.`),
Q(`Why does the I–V graph for a filament lamp curve?`,[`The filament gets hotter, so its resistance increases`,'The current runs out, so it can\'t keep rising','It\'s really a diode, which only lets current one way','The filament gets hotter, so its resistance decreases'],0,`Hotter metal has more resistance, so the current rises less and less.`),
T(`<h3>Sensors</h3><ul><li><b>Thermistor</b>: resistance <b>decreases</b> as temperature <b>increases</b>. Used in thermostats.</li><li><b>LDR</b>: resistance <b>decreases</b> as light gets <b>brighter</b>. Used in automatic night lights.</li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>Series and parallel rules</h3><p><b>Series</b> (one loop): the same current everywhere; the p.d.s add up to the supply p.d.; total resistance R<sub>total</sub> = R₁ + R₂.</p><p><b>Parallel</b> (branches): each branch has the same p.d. as the supply; the branch currents add up to the total current; the total resistance is <b>less than the smallest</b> resistor.</p>`),
W('sortIt',{bins:['Series','Parallel'],items:[['The current is the same everywhere',0],['R<sub>total</sub> = R₁ + R₂',0],['The supply p.d. is shared between the components',0],['Each branch has the same p.d. as the supply',1],['The total current is the sum of the branch currents',1],['Adding another resistor makes the total resistance smaller',1]]},`Series or parallel? Sort the rules.`,`Adding a resistor in parallel gives the current another path, so the total resistance goes down.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_series',need:4},`Series circuit calculations. Get 4 right to master it.`,`Add the resistances first, then use V = IR.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Resistor: straight line. Lamp: curve (resistance rises as it heats). Diode: one way only.</li><li>Thermistor and LDR: resistance falls with heat and with light.</li><li>Series: same current, p.d.s add, resistances add.</li><li>Parallel: same p.d., currents add, total resistance goes down.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp2-2-3','Mains electricity and power',10,'The plug, ac and dc, electrical power and energy, and why the National Grid uses such high voltages.',[
RM(`<p>Series: same current, resistances add. Parallel: same p.d. on each branch. V = IR and Q = It.</p>`),
T(`<h3>ac and dc</h3><p>A battery gives <b>direct current</b> (dc): it flows one way. UK mains is <b>alternating current</b> (ac): it changes direction 50 times a second.</p><p>UK mains: <b>230 V</b>, <b>50 Hz</b>.</p>`),
W('matchPairs',{pairs:[['Live wire','Brown. Carries the alternating p.d. of 230 V'],['Neutral wire','Blue. Completes the circuit, at about 0 V'],['Earth wire','Green and yellow. A safety wire that only carries current if there is a fault']]},`Match each wire in a plug to its job.`,`Brown live, blue neutral, green and yellow earth.`),
Q(`Why is touching the live wire dangerous, even when a switch is off?`,[`It is still at 230 V and you are at 0 V, so a current could flow through you`,'It is much colder than you, so energy flows out of you quickly','It carries dc, which is always more dangerous than ac','It has no plastic coating inside the plug, so it can spark across to your fingers'],0,`A large p.d. between the wire and you (connected to earth) drives a current through your body.`),
T(`<h3>Electrical power and energy</h3><div class="eq">P = V I &nbsp;&nbsp; P = I² R</div><div class="eq">E = P t &nbsp;&nbsp; E = Q V</div>${KY([['P','power','W'],['V','potential difference','V'],['I','current','A'],['R','resistance','Ω'],['E','energy transferred','J'],['Q','charge','C']],'Learn all four.')}`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_pvi',need:4},`Practise power. Get 4 right to master it.`,`P = VI, or P = I²R when you know the resistance.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_eqv',need:3},`Practise E = QV. Get 3 right to master it.`,`p.d. is energy per coulomb of charge.`),
T(`<h3>The National Grid</h3><p>Power stations are linked to homes by the National Grid.</p><ul><li><b>Step-up transformers</b> raise the p.d. to about 400 000 V.</li><li>For the same power (P = VI), a higher p.d. means a <b>lower current</b>.</li><li>A lower current means less energy wasted heating the cables (P = I²R).</li><li><b>Step-down transformers</b> bring it back to 230 V for homes.</li></ul>`),
Q(`Why does the National Grid carry electricity at a very high p.d.?`,[`For the same power, the current is lower, so less energy is wasted heating the cables`,'Electricity at a high p.d. travels faster, so it gets to homes quicker','A high p.d. is safer for homes, so it can be sent straight into the sockets without changing','Power stations can only make high p.d., so it has to stay that way'],0,`Power loss in a cable is I²R. Halving the current quarters the loss.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>UK mains: ac, 230 V, 50 Hz.</li><li>Live brown, neutral blue, earth green and yellow.</li><li><b>P = VI, P = I²R, E = Pt, E = QV</b>.</li><li>High p.d. on the grid means a low current and less waste.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp2-2-4','Static electricity (Physics only)',7,'Separate physics only: how rubbing makes charge, attraction and repulsion, sparks and electric fields.',[
RM(`<p>Current is a flow of charge. In metals the charge carriers are electrons, which are negative.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Charging by friction</h3><p><i>This is in GCSE Physics only, not Combined Science.</i></p><p>Rub two insulators together and <b>electrons</b> are rubbed off one and onto the other. Only electrons move, never protons.</p><ul><li>The one that <b>gains</b> electrons becomes <b>negative</b>.</li><li>The one that <b>loses</b> electrons becomes <b>positive</b>.</li></ul>`),
Q(`A polythene rod is rubbed with a cloth and becomes negative. What happened?`,[`Electrons moved from the cloth to the rod`,`Protons moved to the rod`,`Electrons moved from the rod to the cloth`,`Positive charges were created`],0,`Gaining electrons makes it negative. The cloth is left positive.`),
T(`<h3>Forces and sparks</h3><ul><li><b>Like</b> charges <b>repel</b>; <b>unlike</b> charges <b>attract</b>.</li><li>When enough charge builds up, the p.d. gets high enough for a <b>spark</b> to jump through the air.</li></ul><h3>Electric fields</h3><p>A charged object has an <b>electric field</b> around it. Field lines point away from positive charges and towards negative ones. The field is strongest close to the object.</p>`),
Q(`Two balloons are both rubbed and become negatively charged. What happens when they're held near each other?`,[`They repel`,`They attract`,`Nothing`,`They swap charge and stick`],0,`Like charges repel.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Rubbing moves <b>electrons</b> from one insulator to the other.</li><li>Gained electrons: negative. Lost electrons: positive.</li><li>Like charges repel, unlike attract.</li><li>Field lines go from + to −.</li></ul>`)])
]});

U({id:'gp2-3',track:'gp',level:2,title:'Particle model of matter',icon:'ρ',color:'#1D9E75',desc:'Density, states of matter, internal energy, specific latent heat, heating curves and gas pressure.',lessons:[
LH('gp2-3-1','Density',9,'How tightly packed a material is, the equation, and the density required practical.',[
T(`<h3>Solids, liquids and gases</h3><p>In a <b>solid</b>, particles are packed tightly in a regular pattern and vibrate. In a <b>liquid</b>, they're close together but move past each other. In a <b>gas</b>, they're far apart and move quickly in random directions.</p><p>That's why gases are much less dense than solids and liquids.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Density</h3><div class="eq">ρ = m ÷ V</div>${KY([['ρ','density (ρ is "rho")','kg/m³'],['m','mass','kg'],['V','volume','m³']],'Learn it. Density is often in g/cm³ too: 1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³. Water is 1 g/cm³.')}`),
Q(`Why is a gas much less dense than the same substance as a solid?`,[`Its particles are much further apart`,'Its particles are much lighter than in the solid','Its particles are much smaller than in the solid','It has fewer protons in each of its atoms'],0,`Same particles, same mass each, but spread over a much bigger volume.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_density',need:5},`Practise density. Get 5 right to master it.`,`ρ = m ÷ V, m = ρV, V = m ÷ ρ. Watch the units.`),
T(`<h3>Required practical: density</h3><ul><li><b>Regular solid</b>: weigh it, measure its sides with a ruler, work out the volume.</li><li><b>Irregular solid</b>: weigh it, then lower it into water (a eureka can or measuring cylinder). The volume of water it pushes out is its volume.</li><li><b>Liquid</b>: weigh an empty measuring cylinder, pour in a known volume, weigh again.</li></ul>`),
Q(`How do you find the volume of a small stone?`,[`Lower it into water in a measuring cylinder and see how much the level rises`,'Measure its length, width and height with a ruler and multiply','Weigh it on a balance, then divide the mass by the density of water','Heat it and see how much it expands, then work back to its volume'],0,`Water displacement works for any shape.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li><b>ρ = m ÷ V</b>. Learn it.</li><li>Gases are far less dense: their particles are spread out.</li><li>Irregular objects: use water displacement.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp2-3-2','Changes of state and internal energy',10,'What heating does to particles, why temperature stops rising while things melt, and specific latent heat.',[
RM(`<p>Solid: particles packed and vibrating. Liquid: close but moving past each other. Gas: far apart and fast. Specific heat capacity: ΔE = mcΔθ.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Internal energy</h3><p>The particles in a substance have energy: <b>kinetic</b> (moving) and <b>potential</b> (from the bonds between them). The total is its <b>internal energy</b>.</p><p>Heating raises the internal energy. It either <b>raises the temperature</b> or <b>changes the state</b>, not both at once.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Changes of state</h3><p>Melting, freezing, boiling, evaporating, condensing and sublimating are <b>physical changes</b>. They're reversible and the mass stays the same.</p><p>While a substance melts or boils, its temperature <b>stays the same</b>. The energy goes into breaking the bonds between particles, not into speeding them up. On a heating graph, that's a <b>flat section</b>.</p>`),
Q(`On a heating graph, why is the line flat while ice melts?`,[`The energy is breaking bonds between particles, not speeding them up`,'The heater has stopped giving out energy while the ice melts','The thermometer can\'t measure temperatures below the freezing point','The particles stop moving until all of the ice has turned to water'],0,`The temperature only rises again once it has all melted.`),
T(`<h3>Specific latent heat</h3><p>The energy needed to change the state of <b>1 kg</b> of a substance, <b>without changing its temperature</b>:</p><div class="eq">E = m L</div>${KY([['E','energy for the change of state','J'],['m','mass','kg'],['L','specific latent heat','J/kg']],'On the equation sheet. Fusion = melting or freezing. Vaporisation = boiling or condensing.')}`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_slh',need:3},`Practise E = mL. Get 3 right to master it.`,`L = E ÷ m.`),
Q(`What is the difference between specific heat capacity and specific latent heat?`,[`Heat capacity changes the temperature; latent heat changes the state at constant temperature`,'They\'re the same thing, just measured in different units for solids and liquids','Latent heat changes the temperature; heat capacity changes the state of a gas','Heat capacity is only used for melting solids, and latent heat is only used for boiling water away'],0,`ΔE = mcΔθ when the temperature changes; E = mL when the state changes.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Internal energy = kinetic + potential energy of the particles.</li><li>Changes of state: temperature stays constant, mass is conserved.</li><li><b>E = mL</b> (given): fusion and vaporisation.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp2-3-3','Gas pressure',9,'Why gases push on their containers, what temperature does to pressure, and pV = constant.',[
RM(`<p>Gas particles are far apart and move quickly in random directions. Temperature is linked to how fast particles move.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Where pressure comes from</h3><p>Gas particles are in constant random motion. When they hit the walls of their container they push on them. All those collisions add up to a force on each square metre: the <b>pressure</b>.</p><p>The <b>temperature</b> of a gas is linked to the <b>average kinetic energy</b> of its particles.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Heating a gas in a sealed container</h3><p>If the volume can't change:</p><ol><li>Higher temperature → particles move faster.</li><li>They hit the walls <b>more often</b> and <b>harder</b>.</li><li>So the <b>pressure increases</b>.</li></ol>`),
Q(`A sealed can of gas is heated. Why does the pressure rise?`,[`The particles move faster, so they hit the walls harder and more often`,'The particles get bigger, so they take up more of the can','More particles are made as the gas heats up','The can shrinks as it heats up, so there\'s less space inside for the gas'],0,`Always mention both: more often and with more force.`),
T(`<h3>Squashing a gas (Physics only)</h3><p>At constant temperature, if you squash a gas into a smaller volume the particles hit the walls more often, so the pressure rises:</p><div class="eq">p V = constant</div><p>(on the equation sheet). Halve the volume, double the pressure.</p><p>Doing <b>work</b> on a gas, like pumping a bike tyre, transfers energy to it and <b>raises its temperature</b>.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_boyle',need:3},`Practise pV = constant (Physics only). Get 3 right to master it.`,`p₁V₁ = p₂V₂.`),
Q(`Why does a bike pump get warm when you use it?`,[`Work done on the gas transfers energy to it, raising its temperature`,'Friction from the valve and tyre rubbing against the pump as you push it down','The air is being cooled, which warms the metal','The gas particles slow down and give out their energy'],0,`Pushing the piston does work on the gas particles, speeding them up.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Pressure comes from particles hitting the walls.</li><li>Hotter gas, same volume: higher pressure.</li><li><b>pV = constant</b> at fixed temperature (Physics only).</li><li>Doing work on a gas heats it.</li></ul>`)])
]});

U({id:'gp2-4',track:'gp',level:2,title:'Atomic structure',icon:'☢︎',color:'#7F77DD',desc:'The atom and its history, isotopes, alpha, beta and gamma radiation, nuclear equations, half-life, and (Physics only) fission and fusion.',lessons:[
LH('gp2-4-1','Atoms, isotopes and how we found the nucleus',10,'What an atom is made of, isotopes and ions, and the experiments that changed the model of the atom.',[
T(`<h3>Inside an atom</h3><p>An atom is tiny: about 1 × 10⁻¹⁰ m across. In the middle is the <b>nucleus</b>, about 10 000 times smaller, containing <b>protons</b> and <b>neutrons</b>. <b>Electrons</b> orbit at fixed distances, called <b>energy levels</b>.</p><div class="tbl"><table class="wtable"><tr><th>Particle</th><th>Relative charge</th><th>Relative mass</th></tr><tr><td>Proton</td><td>+1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Neutron</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Electron</td><td>−1</td><td>very small</td></tr></table></div>`),
T(`<h3>Atomic number and mass number</h3><p>A nucleus is written like this: <b>${gNuc('C',14,6)}</b>.</p><ul><li>Bottom: <b>atomic number</b> = number of protons (6, so it's carbon).</li><li>Top: <b>mass number</b> = protons + neutrons (14).</li><li>Neutrons = 14 − 6 = 8.</li></ul><p><b>Isotopes</b> are atoms of the same element (same protons) with different numbers of neutrons. Carbon-12 and carbon-14 are isotopes.</p>`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_isotope',need:4},`Count the protons and neutrons. Get 4 right to master it.`,`Protons = bottom number. Neutrons = top − bottom.`),
T(`<h3>Ions and energy levels</h3><p>Atoms have no overall charge: protons = electrons. If an atom loses an outer electron it becomes a <b>positive ion</b>.</p><p>Electrons can jump to a higher energy level by absorbing electromagnetic radiation, and drop back by giving it out.</p>`),
T(`<h3>How the model changed</h3><p>New evidence changed the model of the atom several times:</p>`),
O(`Put these models of the atom in the order they were developed.`,[`Dalton: atoms are tiny solid spheres that can't be divided`,`Thomson discovers the electron: the "plum pudding" model, a ball of positive charge with electrons in it`,`Rutherford's alpha scattering experiment: a tiny, positive nucleus with mostly empty space around it`,`Bohr: electrons orbit the nucleus at fixed distances (energy levels)`,`Chadwick discovers the neutron (1932)`],`Each model was replaced when an experiment gave results it couldn't explain.`),
Q(`In the alpha scattering experiment, which result showed the nucleus is tiny and positive?`,[`Most alpha particles went straight through, but a very few bounced back`,'All of them went straight through, with none deflected at all','Most of them bounced back, and only a few went straight through','They all spread out evenly in every direction from the foil'],0,`Most went through, so the atom is mostly empty. A few bounced back, so the mass and positive charge are packed into a tiny centre.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Protons (+1, mass 1), neutrons (0, mass 1), electrons (−1, tiny mass).</li><li>Atomic number = protons. Mass number = protons + neutrons.</li><li>Isotopes: same protons, different neutrons.</li><li>Plum pudding → nuclear model (alpha scattering) → Bohr → neutron.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp2-4-2','Radioactive decay and nuclear equations',10,'Why some nuclei are unstable, the three main types of radiation, and how to write nuclear equations.',[
RM(`<p>${gNuc('C',14,6)}: 6 protons, 14 − 6 = 8 neutrons. Isotopes have the same protons, different neutrons.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Unstable nuclei</h3><p>Some nuclei are unstable. They give out radiation to become more stable. This is <b>radioactive decay</b>. It is <b>random</b>: you can't predict which nucleus decays next.</p><p><b>Activity</b> is the rate of decay, in <b>becquerels</b> (Bq): 1 Bq is 1 decay per second. A Geiger-Müller tube measures the <b>count rate</b>.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Alpha, beta and gamma</h3><ul><li><b>Alpha (α)</b>: 2 protons + 2 neutrons (a helium nucleus). Stopped by paper or a few cm of air. <b>Very ionising</b>.</li><li><b>Beta (β)</b>: a fast electron made when a neutron turns into a proton. Stopped by a few mm of aluminium. Moderately ionising.</li><li><b>Gamma (γ)</b>: an electromagnetic wave. Only reduced by thick lead or concrete. <b>Weakly ionising</b>.</li></ul><p>Some nuclei also give out a <b>neutron</b>.</p>`),
W('sortIt',{bins:['Alpha','Beta','Gamma'],items:[['A helium nucleus',0],['Stopped by a sheet of paper',0],['The most ionising',0],['A fast electron from the nucleus',1],['Stopped by a few mm of aluminium',1],['An electromagnetic wave',2],['Needs thick lead to absorb most of it',2],['The least ionising',2]]},`Sort the facts into alpha, beta and gamma.`,`The more ionising it is, the shorter its range.`),
T(`<h3>Nuclear equations</h3><p>In a nuclear equation, the top numbers and bottom numbers must add up the same on both sides.</p><ul><li><b>Alpha</b>: mass number −4, atomic number −2. ${gNuc('U',238,92)} → ${gNuc('Th',234,90)} + ${gNuc('He',4,2)}</li><li><b>Beta</b>: mass number the same, atomic number +1. ${gNuc('C',14,6)} → ${gNuc('N',14,7)} + ${gNuc('e',0,'−1')}</li><li><b>Gamma</b>: no change to either number. It just carries energy away.</li></ul>`),
Q(`A nucleus gives out a beta particle. What happens to its atomic number?`,[`It goes up by 1`,`It goes down by 2`,`It stays the same`,`It goes down by 1`],0,`A neutron becomes a proton, so there is one more proton.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_decay',need:4},`Complete the nuclear equations. Get 4 right to master it.`,`Alpha: −4 and −2. Beta: same and +1.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Decay is random. Activity is measured in Bq.</li><li>α: helium nucleus, paper stops it, very ionising.</li><li>β: electron, aluminium stops it. γ: EM wave, lead reduces it.</li><li>Balance the top and bottom numbers in nuclear equations.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp2-4-3','Half-life and using radiation safely',9,'How long radioactive sources last, working out half-life, and the difference between contamination and irradiation.',[
RM(`<p>Radioactive decay is random. Activity (Bq) is the number of decays per second.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Half-life</h3><p>You can't predict when one nucleus will decay, but with billions of them the pattern is very regular.</p><p>The <b>half-life</b> is the time it takes for the number of unstable nuclei to halve. It's also the time for the <b>count rate</b> (or activity) to halve.</p>`),
W('fnPlot',{x:[0,24],y:[0,1000],xl:'time (hours)',yl:'activity (Bq)',sl:[['h','Half-life',1,8,1,3,function(v){return v+' hours'}]],curves:function(p){return [[function(t){return 1000*Math.pow(0.5,t/p.h)},'--accent',3]]},read:function(p){return 'Half-life '+p.h+' h: after '+p.h+' h the activity is 500 Bq, after '+(2*p.h)+' h it\'s 250 Bq, after '+(3*p.h)+' h it\'s 125 Bq. It never quite reaches zero.'}},`Change the half-life and watch the curve.`,`Every half-life, it halves again.`),
W('calcDrill',{skill:'g_halflife',need:5},`Half-life questions. Get 5 right to master it.`,`Count the halvings. After n half-lives, (½)ⁿ is left.`),
T(`<h3>Contamination and irradiation</h3><ul><li><b>Irradiation</b>: being exposed to radiation from a source outside you. You don't become radioactive.</li><li><b>Contamination</b>: radioactive atoms get on or into something (or someone), so the radiation carries on.</li></ul><p>Stay safe: keep sources far away (use tongs), keep exposure short, use shielding, and wear gloves. Workers wear dosimeters.</p>`),
Q(`What is the difference between contamination and irradiation?`,[`Contamination means radioactive atoms are on or in you; irradiation is exposure from outside`,'They mean the same thing: both are radiation hitting you from outside','Irradiation means radioactive atoms are inside you; contamination makes you radioactive yourself','Contamination only happens with gamma; irradiation only with alpha particles'],0,`Contamination is often more dangerous: the source stays with you.`),
Q(`Inside the body, which type of radiation is most dangerous?`,[`Alpha`,`Beta`,`Gamma`,`They're all the same`],0,`Alpha is the most ionising, and inside the body nothing shields you from it.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Half-life: time for the nuclei (or count rate) to halve.</li><li>After n half-lives, (½)ⁿ remains.</li><li>Irradiation: from outside. Contamination: the atoms are on or in you.</li><li>Distance, time and shielding keep you safe.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('gp2-4-4','Fission, fusion and uses of radiation (Physics only)',10,'Separate physics only: background radiation, radiation in medicine, and the nuclear reactions that power reactors and stars.',[
RM(`<p>Alpha is very ionising with a short range; gamma is weakly ionising with a long range. Half-life is the time for the activity to halve.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Background radiation</h3><p><i>Physics only.</i> We're always exposed to a little radiation. It comes from natural sources: <b>radon gas</b> (the biggest source in the UK, especially in Devon and Cornwall), rocks, and cosmic rays. Smaller amounts come from medical X-rays and old nuclear tests and accidents.</p><p>The <b>dose</b>, measured in <b>sieverts</b> (Sv, usually millisieverts), measures the risk of harm to the body.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Radiation in medicine</h3><ul><li><b>Tracers</b>: a gamma emitter with a short half-life (like technetium-99m) is put into the body and detected from outside. Gamma escapes the body; the short half-life means it doesn't stay active for long.</li><li><b>Radiotherapy</b>: beams of gamma rays from different directions kill cancer cells.</li></ul>`),
Q(`Why is a tracer usually a gamma emitter with a half-life of a few hours?`,[`Gamma can pass out of the body to be detected, and a short half-life means it doesn't stay radioactive for long`,'Gamma is the most ionising, so it\'s easy to detect, and a short half-life makes it cheaper','A long half-life is dangerous to detect, so a short one keeps the detector safe from damage','Alpha can\'t be made in hospitals, and a short half-life means a fresh batch can be made on the morning of the scan'],0,`Alpha would be absorbed inside you and cause damage; it couldn't be detected outside.`),
T(`<h3>Nuclear fission</h3><ol><li>A nucleus of uranium-235 absorbs a slow-moving <b>neutron</b>.</li><li>It becomes unstable and <b>splits</b> into two smaller nuclei (the daughter nuclei).</li><li>It also releases <b>2 or 3 neutrons</b>, gamma rays and a lot of energy.</li><li>Those neutrons can split more nuclei: a <b>chain reaction</b>.</li></ol><p>In a reactor, <b>control rods</b> absorb neutrons to keep it steady. In a bomb, it's uncontrolled. The energy heats water to make steam, which turns turbines, just like at Hinkley Point C.</p>`),
Q(`In a nuclear reactor, what do the control rods do?`,[`Absorb neutrons, to control the rate of fission`,'Speed up the neutrons, to cause more fission','Heat up the uranium, to control the rate of fission','Release extra neutrons, to keep the reaction going'],0,`Fewer neutrons means fewer fissions. Pushing the rods in slows the chain reaction.`),
T(`<h3>Nuclear fusion</h3><p>In <b>fusion</b>, two light nuclei (like hydrogen isotopes) join to make a heavier nucleus. Some mass is converted into energy. It powers the Sun.</p><p>It needs extremely high temperatures and pressures to push the positive nuclei close enough, which is why we can't yet use it in power stations.</p>`),
W('sortIt',{bins:['Fission','Fusion'],items:[['A heavy nucleus splits',0],['Starts when a neutron is absorbed',0],['Used in today\'s nuclear power stations',0],['Releases 2 or 3 neutrons',0],['Two light nuclei join',1],['Powers the Sun',1],['Needs extremely high temperature and pressure',1]]},`Fission or fusion?`,`Fission splits heavy nuclei; fusion joins light ones.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Background radiation: radon is the biggest UK source. Dose is in sieverts.</li><li>Tracers: gamma emitters with short half-lives.</li><li>Fission: U-235 + neutron → two smaller nuclei + 2 or 3 neutrons + energy.</li><li>Control rods absorb neutrons. Fusion joins light nuclei.</li></ul>`)])
]});

/* follow-ups */
F('gp2-1-1',[GN('g_ke'),GN('g_ke'),Q(`Which store does a hot cup of tea mainly have?`,[`Thermal`,`Kinetic`,`Chemical`,`Elastic`],0,`Hot objects: thermal store.`),Q(`Which is NOT a way of transferring energy?`,[`Storing`,`Heating`,`Electrically`,`Mechanically`],0,`Storing isn't a transfer.`),Q(`Triple the speed of an object. Its kinetic energy is multiplied by…`,[`9`,`3`,`6`,`27`],0,`3² = 9.`)]);
F('gp2-1-2',[GN('g_gpe'),GN('g_gpe'),GN('g_epe'),Q(`In E<sub>e</sub> = ½ke², what must the extension be measured in?`,[`metres`,`centimetres`,`millimetres`,`newtons`],0,`Always convert to m.`),Q(`A ball falls with no air resistance. GPE lost = …`,[`KE gained`,`KE lost`,`Twice the KE gained`,`Zero`],0,`Energy is conserved.`)]);
F('gp2-1-3',[GN('g_shc'),GN('g_shc'),GN('g_shc'),Q(`Specific heat capacity is the energy needed to raise the temperature of…`,[`1 kg by 1 °C`,`1 g by 100 °C`,`any mass to boiling`,`1 kg until it melts`],0,`Per kg, per degree.`),Q(`Heat loss in the practical makes the calculated c…`,[`too high`,`too low`,`exactly right`,`zero`],0,`Too much energy for the temperature rise.`)]);
F('gp2-1-4',[GN('g_power'),GN('g_eff'),GN('g_eff'),Q(`What is 1 watt?`,[`1 joule per second`,`1 joule per minute`,`1 newton per metre`,`1 volt per amp`],0,`Power is energy per second.`),Q(`Which is a renewable resource?`,[`Geothermal`,`Natural gas`,`Coal`,`Uranium`],0,`Heat from inside the Earth keeps coming.`)]);
F('gp2-2-1',[GN('g_vir'),GN('g_vir'),GN('g_charge'),Q(`An ammeter is connected…`,[`in series`,`in parallel`,`across the battery only`,`either way`],0,`Current passes through it.`),Q(`A longer wire has…`,[`more resistance`,`less resistance`,`the same resistance`,`no resistance`],0,`Resistance is proportional to length.`)]);
F('gp2-2-2',[GN('g_series'),GN('g_series'),Q(`The resistance of a thermistor ___ as it gets hotter.`,[`decreases`,`increases`,`stays the same`,`becomes infinite`],0,`Used in thermostats.`),Q(`In parallel, adding another resistor makes the total resistance…`,[`smaller`,`bigger`,`the same`,`zero`],0,`Another path for the current.`),Q(`Which component only lets current flow one way?`,[`Diode`,`Resistor`,`Lamp`,`Thermistor`],0,`Very high resistance in reverse.`)]);
F('gp2-2-3',[GN('g_pvi'),GN('g_pvi'),GN('g_eqv'),Q(`What colour is the earth wire?`,[`Green and yellow`,`Brown`,`Blue`,`Red`],0,`Safety wire.`),Q(`UK mains frequency is…`,[`50 Hz`,`230 Hz`,`60 Hz`,`100 Hz`],0,`230 V at 50 Hz.`)]);
F('gp2-2-4',[Q(`Rubbing moves which particles?`,[`Electrons`,`Protons`,`Neutrons`,`Nuclei`],0,`Only electrons move.`),Q(`An object that loses electrons becomes…`,[`positive`,`negative`,`neutral`,`magnetic`],0,`Fewer negatives.`),Q(`Unlike charges…`,[`attract`,`repel`,`do nothing`,`cancel out and disappear`],0,`Opposites attract.`),Q(`Electric field lines point…`,[`away from positive charges`,`towards positive charges`,`in circles`,`nowhere`],0,`+ to −.`),Q(`A spark happens when…`,[`the p.d. gets high enough`,`the charge runs out`,`the object gets cold`,`protons jump`],0,`Charge builds up until the air conducts.`)]);
F('gp2-3-1',[GN('g_density'),GN('g_density'),GN('g_density'),Q(`1 g/cm³ is how many kg/m³?`,[`1000`,`1`,`100`,`0.001`],0,`Water: 1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³.`),Q(`Which state has the lowest density?`,[`Gas`,`Solid`,`Liquid`,`They're all the same`],0,`Particles far apart.`)]);
F('gp2-3-2',[GN('g_slh'),GN('g_slh'),Q(`While water boils, its temperature…`,[`stays the same`,`rises quickly`,`falls`,`goes up and down`],0,`Energy breaks bonds.`),Q(`Internal energy is the total of the particles'…`,[`kinetic and potential energy`,`kinetic energy only`,`mass`,`temperature`],0,`Both.`),Q(`Specific latent heat of fusion is for…`,[`melting or freezing`,`boiling`,`heating a solid`,`cooling a gas`],0,`Fusion = melting.`)]);
F('gp2-3-3',[GN('g_boyle'),Q(`Gas pressure is caused by…`,[`particles colliding with the walls`,`particles sticking to the walls`,`the weight of the container`,`heat escaping`],0,`Collisions.`),Q(`A gas is heated at constant volume. Its pressure…`,[`increases`,`decreases`,`stays the same`,`becomes zero`],0,`Faster particles.`),Q(`Temperature of a gas is linked to the particles'…`,[`average kinetic energy`,`size`,`number of protons`,`colour`],0,`Faster means hotter.`),Q(`Halve the volume of a gas at constant temperature. The pressure…`,[`doubles`,`halves`,`stays the same`,`quadruples`],0,`pV = constant.`)]);
F('gp2-4-1',[GN('g_isotope'),GN('g_isotope'),Q(`Isotopes have different numbers of…`,[`neutrons`,`protons`,`electrons only`,`nuclei`],0,`Same element, same protons.`),Q(`Who discovered the neutron?`,[`Chadwick`,`Thomson`,`Dalton`,`Bohr`],0,`In 1932.`),Q(`Most alpha particles went straight through the gold foil. This showed…`,[`the atom is mostly empty space`,`the atom is solid`,`electrons are big`,`the nucleus is negative`],0,`Mostly empty.`)]);
F('gp2-4-2',[GN('g_decay'),GN('g_decay'),Q(`Which radiation is an electromagnetic wave?`,[`Gamma`,`Alpha`,`Beta`,`Neutron`],0,`No mass, no charge.`),Q(`What stops alpha radiation?`,[`A sheet of paper`,`Thick lead only`,`Nothing`,`A few mm of aluminium only`],0,`Very short range.`),Q(`1 becquerel is…`,[`1 decay per second`,`1 decay per minute`,`1 sievert`,`1 count per hour`],0,`Activity unit.`)]);
F('gp2-4-3',[GN('g_halflife'),GN('g_halflife'),GN('g_halflife'),Q(`Contamination means…`,[`radioactive atoms are on or in something`,`being exposed from a distance`,`a type of decay`,`a shield`],0,`The source stays with you.`),Q(`Radioactive decay is…`,[`random`,`predictable for each nucleus`,`caused by heating`,`always alpha`],0,`You can only predict large numbers.`)]);
F('gp2-4-4',[Q(`What starts a fission reaction in uranium-235?`,[`Absorbing a slow neutron`,`Heating it`,`Absorbing an electron`,`Losing a proton`],0,`Then it splits.`),Q(`How many neutrons does each fission typically release?`,[`2 or 3`,`0`,`1`,`10`],0,`They can cause more fissions.`),Q(`Fusion joins…`,[`light nuclei`,`heavy nuclei`,`electrons`,`molecules`],0,`Like hydrogen isotopes in the Sun.`),Q(`The biggest source of background radiation in the UK is…`,[`radon gas`,`nuclear power stations`,`mobile phones`,`X-rays`],0,`From rocks, especially in the south-west.`),Q(`Dose is measured in…`,[`sieverts`,`becquerels`,`joules`,`ohms`],0,`Sv or mSv.`)]);
